#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Renders a Claude Code session transcript as a simple prompt / answer page.

Which sessions you get depends on where you run it. The project is that of the Claude Code session it is
run from (e.g. with its `!` prefix), else the current directory (where `claude` was started), or else the
nearest directory above it that is one; -p/--project picks another. You get all its sessions; with
-s/--session only the most recently active one (when run from Claude Code, the session you are in), or
with -s ID the most recent one whose id starts with ID. -r/--recursive also renders the projects in the
directories below the project's (or below the current directory, even if it is not one), at most
-d/--depth levels down (3 by default; 0: no limit); with -s, the latest session of each. -w/--watch picks
again on every pass, so it follows new sessions (unless -s was given an id, or picked the session you are
in). With -e/--serve, the server only shows the rendered sessions of those projects (including ones
rendered by earlier runs), or with -s only the sessions rendered this time; a later -e run using the same
server adds its own. Pages opened as files are not limited like that: their index
(~/.claude/conversations/index.html) lists every session rendered so far, from any project.

Reads the session's JSONL under ~/.claude/projects/<project-slug>/ and writes an HTML page: a numbered
list of your prompts (sidebar, with a search box; a button hides it and shows it again, remembered across
pages) and, for each, the assistant's final answer rendered from markdown with a "copy" button;
intermediate assistant text (progress notes between tool calls, replies to background notifications) is
folded under each turn. Tool calls, tool results and thinking are left out. Stdlib only.

Usage (e.g. with Claude Code's `!` prefix, whose output is added to the conversation's context; with
respondToBashCommands false in settings.json Claude does not reply to it):
    claude-conversation                 # all sessions of this project -> HTML, prints the index's URL
    claude-conversation -s              # only this session (else this directory's latest), printing its URL
    claude-conversation -o              # ... and open the printed URL in the browser (--open)
    claude-conversation -s <id-prefix> | -r | -j <file>   # other sessions (see above)
    claude-conversation -w -o           # regenerate every 30 s (-w SEC: every SEC s) until Ctrl+C (--watch)
    claude-conversation -e              # ... and print an http:// URL instead (--serve; see below)
    claude-conversation --stop-server
Output: ~/.claude/conversations/<project-slug>/<session-id>.html, and next to it <session-id>.search.js
(its prompts and answers, for searches), <session-id>.marks.js and <session-id>.version.js. Every run
also rewrites ~/.claude/conversations/index.html, which links every session page under that directory
(all projects, grouped by project); its search box searches all their prompts and answers, listing the
matches under each session. When a session page's search has no match, it lists the other sessions that
have one, linking to the same search there (from ~/.claude/conversations/sessions.js, also rewritten
every run). Only one URL is printed: the page's, or the index's if several sessions were rendered (and,
with -w, then the URL of each session that shows up later). With -v, one line per session is printed
instead (HTML path, the session's name as `/resume` lists it, and its prompt count), then the index URL.
--open uses xdg-open on the printed URL, so it does nothing useful over SSH: open the URL yourself
instead. Each page starts with a title line, "claude conversations" (linking to the index from session
pages), followed by when it was generated and, when served, the address it was reached at, the host name
and the server's pid.

At its top right, each prompt has "copy", "hide" and star buttons, and each answer "copy" and a star.
Either star stars (or unstars) the turn, and "hide" hides the turn (prompt and answer) or shows it again.
The prompt list shows starred turns first, unless its "starred first" box is unticked. Hidden turns are
left out of the page, its prompt list and its searches, and of the index's search, unless "show hidden"
is ticked (on either page; the turns are then dimmed); next to it is how many turns are hidden (on the
index, in the sessions it lists). The --serve server saves stars and hidden turns (pages opened as files
show them but cannot change them), with their prompts and answers, to ~/.claude/conversations/.starred.json
and .hidden.json, whose entries later runs keep up to date with the transcript (e.g. an answer that was
still being written).

-e/--serve starts a background HTTP server for ~/.claude/conversations on all interfaces (port 10041 by
default; --port), unless it is already running, and prints http:// URLs instead of file:// ones. Their
host is the address this machine was reached at over SSH (from $SSH_CONNECTION, so the link works from
the SSH client's browser), else localhost. The server only serves the sessions of the run's projects, or
with -s / -j only those sessions (see above). Runs sharing a server (the same port) add theirs to those
it shows, and its index lists them all, until it stops. A run without -e only warns when a server is
running that does not show its pages. The server has no access control: anyone who can reach the port can
read what it shows. When it cannot be reached, pages viewed through it say so below their title line, with
the command (and directory) of the run that started it, to restart it (with that run's sessions only).
The server runs until --stop-server, or until it has gone 30 min with no page open and no
`claude-conversation -w` running. A running server from an older version of this script is replaced.
Ctrl+C in a -w -e run in a terminal asks what to do: stop the server and exit (the default, also on
another Ctrl+C), "n" to carry on watching (the question is then cleared), or "b" to exit and leave the
server running in the background. When another -w -e run uses the same server, the question says so and
the default (also on another Ctrl+C) is "b" instead, so the other run keeps its server; "y" stops it.
"""
